# Each of these spawns a real `node` and asserts on what the child printed or
# what status it exited with. The child is node running node's own `process`,
# so the assertion is about node's binary; there is no way to install our
# module into a process we did not start.
#
# `test-process-env.js` and `test-process-kill-null.js` also spawn, but only
# after testing in-process behaviour that is ours, so they are left in and fail
# on the spawned half rather than being excluded whole.
test-process-argv-0.js: spawns node to see what argv[0] the child reports
test-process-exec-argv.js: spawns node with flags and reads back execArgv
test-process-execpath.js: spawns node at its own execPath
test-process-ppid.js: spawns node and compares the child's ppid to our pid
test-process-execve-abort.js: spawns node to watch a process image be replaced
test-process-remove-all-signal-listeners.js: spawns node and signals it
test-process-really-exit.js: spawns node to observe a monkey-patched exit
test-process-uncaught-exception-monitor.js: spawns node to read its exit status
test-process-raw-debug.js: spawns node and reads the child's raw stderr
test-process-exit-after-fetch-throw.js: spawns node and reads its exit code
test-process-exit-code.js: spawns node once per exit-code case
test-process-exit-code-validation.js: spawns node once per invalid exit code
test-process-external-stdio-close.js: spawns node with its stdio closed
test-process-external-stdio-close-spawn.js: spawns node with its stdio closed
test-process-getgroups.js: shells out to `id -G` and compares
test-process-redirect-warnings.js: spawns node with --redirect-warnings
test-process-redirect-warnings-env.js: spawns node with NODE_REDIRECT_WARNINGS

# `execve` replaces the process image. A test that calls it successfully does
# not fail the runner, it *ends* it -- the kernel loads another program over
# this one, and whatever the runner still had to report goes with it. There is
# no in-process form of that. `test-process-execve-validation.js` is left in:
# every one of its calls is expected to throw before reaching the system call.
test-process-execve.js: replaces the process image, taking the runner with it
test-process-execve-no-args.js: replaces the process image
test-process-execve-on-exit.js: replaces the process image from an exit handler
test-process-execve-socket.js: replaces the process image with an open socket
test-process-execve-permission-fail.js: needs --permission to deny the call
test-process-execve-permission-granted.js: needs --permission to allow the call
test-process-execve-worker-threads.js: needs worker_threads to be the caller
